      "text": "To find the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we first need to determine the projection formula. The projection of a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}} \\mathbf{a}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). First, we calculate \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = (-3)^2 + (-2)^2 = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the dot product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\\) where \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = (-3)x + (-2)y = -3x - 2y\n\\]\n\nThus, the projection formula becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{-3x - 2y}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9x + 6y}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6x + 4y}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis can be written in matrix form as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), we need to check if the matrix is invertible. A matrix is invertible if and only if its determinant is non-zero. The determinant of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{P}) = \\left( \\frac{9}{13} \\right) \\left( \\frac{4}{13} \\right) - \\left( \\frac{6}{13} \\right) \\left( \\frac{6}{13} \\right) = \\frac{36}{169} - \\frac{36}{169} = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the determinant is zero, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is not invertible. Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) does not exist.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
